1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,906
Take a look at this sum.

2
00:00:01,956 --> 00:00:08,519
There are six terms, and each one is a product
of two differences, like y₁ − y₂.

3
00:00:08,917 --> 00:00:14,151
Some of these differences deserve to be called
positive, and some negative.

4
00:00:14,201 --> 00:00:18,774
I'll color them green and red, and in a few
minutes you'll see the rule.

5
00:00:18,824 --> 00:00:22,443
For now, just notice that the terms are a mix
of both.

6
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:28,259
But add everything up, and the whole
expression collapses into a single product.

7
00:00:28,309 --> 00:00:30,563
And both of its factors are green.

8
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:34,262
That's no coincidence.

9
00:00:34,312 --> 00:00:38,970
A theorem from geometry promises that this
always happens.

10
00:00:39,020 --> 00:00:43,358
But geometry only tells us that the total is
positive.

11
00:00:43,408 --> 00:00:45,961
It doesn't show us why, term by term.

12
00:00:46,350 --> 00:00:52,992
Finding a formula where every single term is
visibly positive is a classic quest in

13
00:00:52,992 --> 00:00:54,912
algebraic combinatorics.

14
00:00:54,962 --> 00:01:00,310
In this video, I want to show you one such
problem, about the double Edelman-Greene

15
00:01:00,310 --> 00:01:03,339
coefficients, and how a recent paper solves
it.

16
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:08,151
Let's start with permutations.

17
00:01:08,201 --> 00:01:14,664
A permutation is just a rearrangement of the
numbers one through n, which we write as a

18
00:01:14,664 --> 00:01:15,035
list.

19
00:01:15,085 --> 00:01:20,539
For example, 2143 swaps the first two numbers,
and the last two.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:26,767
To every permutation w, there's a polynomial
called the Schubert polynomial.

21
00:01:26,817 --> 00:01:32,841
These come from geometry, where they describe
the cohomology of flag varieties.

22
00:01:32,891 --> 00:01:35,977
But today, we only need their combinatorics.

23
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:42,190
In fact, we'll use double Schubert
polynomials, which have two sets of variables,

24
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:43,627
the x's and the y's.

25
00:01:43,677 --> 00:01:48,697
Every term is a product of differences, xᵢ −
yⱼ.

26
00:01:48,747 --> 00:01:51,492
Here is the one for 2143.

27
00:01:51,883 --> 00:01:54,212
Where do these terms come from?

28
00:01:54,262 --> 00:01:57,689
There's a beautiful picture for that, called
pipe dreams.

29
00:01:58,383 --> 00:02:00,865
Take a staircase shaped grid.

30
00:02:00,915 --> 00:02:06,326
In each square, place one of two tiles: a
cross, or a pair of bumping elbows.

31
00:02:06,716 --> 00:02:12,590
Pipes enter from the left, one in each row,
and travel until they leave through the top.

32
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:17,661
We want the pipe entering row i to exit at
column w(i).

33
00:02:17,711 --> 00:02:21,181
And there's one rule: two pipes may never
cross twice.

34
00:02:21,566 --> 00:02:26,465
For the permutation 132, there are exactly two
such pipe dreams.

35
00:02:26,850 --> 00:02:33,449
Each cross in row i and column j contributes a
factor, xᵢ − yⱼ.

36
00:02:33,499 --> 00:02:40,760
So the first pipe dream gives x₁ − y₂, and the
second gives x₂ − y₁.

37
00:02:40,810 --> 00:02:43,640
Their sum is the double Schubert polynomial.

38
00:02:44,033 --> 00:02:50,362
For 2143, there are three pipe dreams,
matching the three terms we saw before.

39
00:02:51,550 --> 00:02:57,424
There's a second, very different picture,
found by Lam, Lee, and Shimozono: bumpless

40
00:02:57,424 --> 00:02:58,263
pipe dreams.

41
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:00,791
Now the grid is a square.

42
00:03:00,841 --> 00:03:06,480
Pipes enter from the bottom and leave through
the right side, using these six tiles.

43
00:03:06,530 --> 00:03:10,341
There's no bumping tile anymore, but there is
an empty one.

44
00:03:10,733 --> 00:03:13,770
Again, no two pipes may cross twice.

45
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:17,368
And this time, the weight comes from the empty
squares.

46
00:03:17,418 --> 00:03:22,488
An empty square in row i and column j gives xᵢ
− yⱼ.

47
00:03:22,883 --> 00:03:26,602
For 132, there are again two diagrams.

48
00:03:26,652 --> 00:03:31,125
They give x₂ − y₂, and x₁ − y₁.

49
00:03:31,517 --> 00:03:34,242
Different terms, but the same total!

50
00:03:34,292 --> 00:03:37,692
Both pictures compute the same polynomial.

51
00:03:37,742 --> 00:03:40,416
Keep this little miracle in mind.

52
00:03:40,466 --> 00:03:43,274
It will come back later as one of our tools.

53
00:03:44,467 --> 00:03:50,923
Next, Lam, Lee, and Shimozono asked: what
happens if we keep adding fixed points to the

54
00:03:50,923 --> 00:03:52,630
front of a permutation?

55
00:03:53,017 --> 00:03:59,338
Put a new number in front of 132, and shift
the labels so that the new number is called

56
00:03:59,338 --> 00:03:59,702
zero.

57
00:03:59,752 --> 00:04:06,596
The grid grows by one row and one column, and
there's a new diagram, with an empty square at

58
00:04:06,596 --> 00:04:07,787
position (0, 0).

59
00:04:08,184 --> 00:04:13,418
So the polynomial picks up a new term, x₀ −
y₀.

60
00:04:13,468 --> 00:04:18,239
Do it again, and we pick up x₋₁ − y₋₁.

61
00:04:18,634 --> 00:04:20,455
Repeat this forever.

62
00:04:20,505 --> 00:04:24,651
In the limit, we get the back-stable double
Schubert polynomial.

63
00:04:24,701 --> 00:04:35,424
For 132, it's x₂ − y₂, plus x₁ − y₁, plus the
sum of xᵢ − yᵢ, over every i up to zero.

64
00:04:35,817 --> 00:04:41,817
So now our variables are indexed by all the
integers, and the grid stretches infinitely

65
00:04:41,817 --> 00:04:43,404
far up and to the left.

66
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:47,807
Some back-stable polynomials are special.

67
00:04:47,857 --> 00:04:53,627
The double Schur functions, introduced by
Molev, have their own bumpless pipe dream

68
00:04:53,627 --> 00:04:59,050
formula, where everything happens above the
line between row zero and row one.

69
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:01,847
They're indexed by partitions.

70
00:05:01,897 --> 00:05:05,296
To see why, look at where the pipes cross that
line.

71
00:05:05,346 --> 00:05:11,444
Reading from left to right, each pipe tells us
to step up, and each gap tells us to step

72
00:05:11,444 --> 00:05:11,860
right.

73
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:17,336
Far to the left, every column has a pipe, and
far to the right, none do.

74
00:05:17,386 --> 00:05:21,688
So the path traces out a Young diagram: the
partition, lambda.

75
00:05:22,083 --> 00:05:27,360
Just like ordinary Schur functions, double
Schur functions form a basis.

76
00:05:27,410 --> 00:05:33,135
So we can expand any back-stable double
Schubert polynomial in terms of them, and the

77
00:05:33,135 --> 00:05:35,829
coefficients are polynomials in x and y.

78
00:05:36,217 --> 00:05:39,595
Now, set every xᵢ equal to yᵢ.

79
00:05:39,645 --> 00:05:43,706
What's left are polynomials in the y variables
alone.

80
00:05:43,756 --> 00:05:46,842
These are the double Edelman-Greene
coefficients.

81
00:05:47,233 --> 00:05:53,363
When all the y's are zero, they become the
classical Edelman-Greene coefficients, which

82
00:05:53,363 --> 00:05:54,984
count certain tableaux.

83
00:05:55,034 --> 00:05:58,050
So those are nonnegative integers.

84
00:05:58,100 --> 00:06:00,631
What can we say about the double version?

85
00:06:01,817 --> 00:06:06,112
Lam, Lee, and Shimozono proved a positivity
theorem.

86
00:06:06,162 --> 00:06:12,718
Every double Edelman-Greene coefficient is a
polynomial with nonnegative integer

87
00:06:12,718 --> 00:06:19,766
coefficients, in the differences yᵢ − yⱼ,
where i comes before j, in a peculiar order.

88
00:06:20,150 --> 00:06:21,928
Here is the order.

89
00:06:21,978 --> 00:06:26,572
Take the integers, and pull all the positive
ones to the front.

90
00:06:26,622 --> 00:06:31,109
First comes one, then two, then three, and so
on forever.

91
00:06:31,159 --> 00:06:37,680
Only after all of them come the rest: minus
two, minus one, and zero at the very end.

92
00:06:38,067 --> 00:06:43,536
So y₁ − y₃ is positive, since one comes before
three.

93
00:06:43,586 --> 00:06:47,348
y₃ − y₀ is positive too.

94
00:06:47,398 --> 00:06:51,956
But y₂ − y₁ is negative, since two comes after
one.

95
00:06:52,350 --> 00:06:55,365
This explains the colors from the beginning.

96
00:06:55,415 --> 00:07:03,188
That six term sum was the coefficient for w
equals 1432, with lambda a single box.

97
00:07:03,238 --> 00:07:08,585
Its value is y₁ − y₃, times y₃ − y₀.

98
00:07:08,984 --> 00:07:11,487
But their proof is geometric.

99
00:07:11,537 --> 00:07:18,392
So they asked for a combinatorial proof: a
formula where every single term is visibly

100
00:07:18,392 --> 00:07:19,118
positive.

101
00:07:19,168 --> 00:07:20,355
That's the problem.

102
00:07:21,550 --> 00:07:26,336
The solution uses three weapons, each forged
in earlier work.

103
00:07:26,386 --> 00:07:29,572
A sword, from Lam, Lee, and Shimozono.

104
00:07:29,622 --> 00:07:32,000
A pair of glasses, from Lenart and Sottile.

105
00:07:33,050 --> 00:07:35,383
And a gauntlet, from Samuel.

106
00:07:35,433 --> 00:07:37,751
Let's pick them up, one at a time.

107
00:07:38,950 --> 00:07:40,664
First, the sword.

108
00:07:40,714 --> 00:07:44,269
Its power is to cut bumpless pipe dreams into
pieces.

109
00:07:44,667 --> 00:07:49,944
Here's a bumpless pipe dream for the back-
stable version of 2143.

110
00:07:49,994 --> 00:07:53,848
Let's cut it along the line between row zero
and row one.

111
00:07:54,233 --> 00:08:00,272
The top piece is exactly a diagram for a
double Schur function, and the pipes crossing

112
00:08:00,272 --> 00:08:02,027
the cut spell out lambda.

113
00:08:02,077 --> 00:08:04,352
Here, lambda is a single box.

114
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:07,381
The bottom piece is a rectangle.

115
00:08:07,431 --> 00:08:13,210
Summing over all the rectangles with the same
top boundary gives the coefficient of the

116
00:08:13,210 --> 00:08:14,671
double Schur function.

117
00:08:14,721 --> 00:08:22,836
And once we set x equal to y, each empty
square in row i and column j contributes yᵢ −

118
00:08:22,836 --> 00:08:23,119
yⱼ.

119
00:08:23,517 --> 00:08:29,092
For 2143, and lambda a single box, there are
four rectangles.

120
00:08:29,142 --> 00:08:36,296
One has an empty square on the diagonal, which
gives y₃ − y₃, which is zero.

121
00:08:36,346 --> 00:08:43,379
The others give y₁ − y₂, y₂ − y₁, and y₁ − y₀.

122
00:08:43,767 --> 00:08:47,806
So the coefficient is y₁ − y₀.

123
00:08:47,856 --> 00:08:49,997
Positive, as promised.

124
00:08:50,047 --> 00:08:51,889
But look at the terms.

125
00:08:51,939 --> 00:08:55,786
There's a red one, and it cancels against a
green one.

126
00:08:55,836 --> 00:08:59,093
This formula is not positive term by term.

127
00:08:59,483 --> 00:09:01,918
Where do red squares come from?

128
00:09:01,968 --> 00:09:05,666
Let's color the rectangle by the sign of each
square.

129
00:09:05,716 --> 00:09:10,908
Above the diagonal, j is bigger than i, so
every square is green.

130
00:09:10,958 --> 00:09:16,362
To the left of column one, j is at most zero,
so those are green too.

131
00:09:16,412 --> 00:09:22,577
The trouble lives only in the lower left
corner, where j is positive, but smaller than

132
00:09:22,577 --> 00:09:22,720
i.

133
00:09:23,117 --> 00:09:27,455
So let's swing the sword a second time, along
the diagonal.

134
00:09:27,505 --> 00:09:32,888
The rectangle splits into a trapezoid on the
left, and a triangle on the right.

135
00:09:32,938 --> 00:09:39,295
The coefficient becomes a sum over the ways
pipes cross the diagonal, of a trapezoid part

136
00:09:39,295 --> 00:09:40,867
times a triangle part.

137
00:09:41,267 --> 00:09:47,312
The triangle part is always positive, so all
the difficulty is in the trapezoids.

138
00:09:47,362 --> 00:09:53,705
In our example, the red term and one green
term share the same trapezoid boundary.

139
00:09:53,755 --> 00:09:57,474
Together, they add up to y₂ − y₀.

140
00:09:57,524 --> 00:09:58,948
Positive!

141
00:09:58,998 --> 00:10:00,655
But only after adding.

142
00:10:01,050 --> 00:10:03,361
So here's the real question.

143
00:10:03,411 --> 00:10:06,679
Why is every trapezoid sum positive?

144
00:10:06,729 --> 00:10:08,961
And can we see it, term by term?

145
00:10:10,150 --> 00:10:12,077
Time for the glasses.

146
00:10:12,127 --> 00:10:17,347
With them, Lenart and Sottile could see pipes
as chains, in the Bruhat order.

147
00:10:17,733 --> 00:10:20,812
The Bruhat order compares permutations.

148
00:10:20,862 --> 00:10:27,177
We go up one step by swapping two entries,
where the smaller one comes first, as long as

149
00:10:27,177 --> 00:10:30,406
no entry between them has a value in between.

150
00:10:30,456 --> 00:10:33,734
Here's the Bruhat order on permutations of
three.

151
00:10:34,133 --> 00:10:37,084
Now draw a bar after position k.

152
00:10:37,134 --> 00:10:42,889
An increasing k-chain is a sequence of such
swaps, each one swapping a number left of the

153
00:10:42,889 --> 00:10:48,839
bar with a number right of the bar, where the
smaller numbers being swapped keep increasing.

154
00:10:49,233 --> 00:10:54,339
For example, start from 413|2765.

155
00:10:54,389 --> 00:10:58,791
We swap one and two, then four and six, then
six and seven.

156
00:10:58,841 --> 00:11:02,091
The smaller numbers are one, four, and six.

157
00:11:02,141 --> 00:11:05,483
They increase, so this is an increasing
3-chain.

158
00:11:05,883 --> 00:11:08,024
Each chain gets a weight.

159
00:11:08,074 --> 00:11:12,817
Look to the right of the bar, at the entries
that end where they started.

160
00:11:12,867 --> 00:11:20,412
Each fixed entry, with value v, contributes a
factor z − yᵥ, where z is a variable we attach

161
00:11:20,412 --> 00:11:21,478
to the chain.

162
00:11:21,867 --> 00:11:29,254
The theorem of Lenart and Sottile says that
pipe dreams correspond exactly to sequences of

163
00:11:29,254 --> 00:11:36,476
chains: a 1-chain, then a 2-chain, and so on,
all the way up to the longest permutation,

164
00:11:36,476 --> 00:11:38,856
labeled by x₁, x₂, and so on.

165
00:11:38,906 --> 00:11:40,178
And the weights match.

166
00:11:40,567 --> 00:11:49,822
For 132, the two pipe dreams become these two
chain sequences, with weights x₁ − y₂, and x₂

167
00:11:49,822 --> 00:11:50,330
− y₁.

168
00:11:50,717 --> 00:11:56,993
Yu found the same kind of picture for bumpless
pipe dreams, except the labels run in the

169
00:11:56,993 --> 00:11:58,063
opposite order.

170
00:11:58,113 --> 00:12:02,927
Here, they give x₂ − y₂, and x₁ − y₁.

171
00:12:03,317 --> 00:12:06,268
And the glasses work for trapezoids too.

172
00:12:06,318 --> 00:12:10,635
Here's a trapezoid boundary with exactly three
fillings.

173
00:12:10,685 --> 00:12:18,714
Their weights are y₃ − y₂, y₂ − y₀, and y₁ −
y₋₁.

174
00:12:18,764 --> 00:12:20,250
The first one is red.

175
00:12:20,633 --> 00:12:27,237
Through the glasses, reading from the bottom
row to the top, each filling becomes a

176
00:12:27,237 --> 00:12:31,136
sequence of chains, labeled y₃, then y₂, then
y₁.

177
00:12:31,186 --> 00:12:34,436
The empty square becomes the yellow entry.

178
00:12:34,486 --> 00:12:38,618
The green entries come from the triangle, and
get divided away.

179
00:12:39,817 --> 00:12:43,174
The final weapon is the gauntlet of Samuel.

180
00:12:43,224 --> 00:12:46,992
Its power is to swap the labels in a sequence
of chains.

181
00:12:47,384 --> 00:12:54,034
Samuel showed that if you sum over all chains,
an a-chain followed by a b-chain gives the

182
00:12:54,034 --> 00:12:57,546
same total as a b-chain followed by an
a-chain.

183
00:12:57,596 --> 00:13:01,919
So the labels can be reordered freely, without
changing the sum.

184
00:13:02,317 --> 00:13:04,786
We've already seen this in action!

185
00:13:04,836 --> 00:13:10,720
Pipe dreams read the labels in increasing
order, and bumpless pipe dreams read them in

186
00:13:10,720 --> 00:13:11,884
decreasing order.

187
00:13:11,934 --> 00:13:16,726
The gauntlet explains why both pictures always
give the same polynomial.

188
00:13:17,117 --> 00:13:24,706
Now let's put on the gauntlet, and reverse the
labels of our trapezoid chains: y₁ first, then

189
00:13:24,706 --> 00:13:25,686
y₂, then y₃.

190
00:13:26,084 --> 00:13:30,934
There are still three chain sequences, and the
total hasn't changed.

191
00:13:30,984 --> 00:13:41,039
But now, the weights are y₃ − y₋₁, then y₂ −
y₂, which is zero, and y₁ − y₀.

192
00:13:41,089 --> 00:13:43,258
Every term is green, or zero!

193
00:13:45,083 --> 00:13:47,096
And that's no accident.

194
00:13:47,146 --> 00:13:53,815
The key proposition: after reversing the
labels, each chain sequence coming from a

195
00:13:53,815 --> 00:14:01,297
trapezoid has weight either zero, or a product
of factors yᵢ − yⱼ, where i is positive and j

196
00:14:01,297 --> 00:14:02,598
is at most zero.

197
00:14:02,648 --> 00:14:04,902
Every such factor is positive.

198
00:14:05,300 --> 00:14:08,038
So let's put the three weapons together.

199
00:14:08,088 --> 00:14:12,725
The sword cuts each coefficient into
trapezoids and triangles.

200
00:14:12,775 --> 00:14:16,003
The glasses turn trapezoids into chains.

201
00:14:16,053 --> 00:14:19,047
And the gauntlet reverses their labels.

202
00:14:19,097 --> 00:14:25,012
The result is a formula for the double
Edelman-Greene coefficients where every single

203
00:14:25,012 --> 00:14:26,194
term is positive.

204
00:14:26,583 --> 00:14:32,670
This is exactly the combinatorial proof that
Lam, Lee, and Shimozono asked for.

205
00:14:32,720 --> 00:14:38,391
And the same formula even works for
permutations of all the integers, giving a

206
00:14:38,391 --> 00:14:42,462
combinatorial proof of a positivity theorem of
Anderson.

207
00:14:43,650 --> 00:14:45,620
There's more to explore.

208
00:14:45,670 --> 00:14:48,041
Is there a K-theory version?

209
00:14:48,091 --> 00:14:51,444
What about triple Edelman-Greene coefficients?

210
00:14:51,494 --> 00:14:57,052
And could the glasses and the gauntlet shed
light on Schubert structure constants, one of

211
00:14:57,052 --> 00:14:59,487
the central open problems in the field?

212
00:14:59,883 --> 00:15:03,944
If you'd like the details, the paper is on the
arXiv.

213
00:15:03,994 --> 00:15:05,309
Thanks for watching!
